===== WIP OpenWrt Install =====
{{page>meta:infobox:dangerous&noheader&nofooter&noeditbtn}}
From this [[https://forum.openwrt.org/viewtopic.php?pid=329810#p329810|forum post]] about the [[toh:hootoo:ht-tm06|HT-TM06]]; works on the HT-TM05 as well.


**WARNING: We haven't found a way to revert back to HooToo's stock firmware yet! If you follow this process, <color #ed1c24>it's permanent!</color>**
In order to revert back, you will have to send it in for service. Although HooToo Support has been very gracious with a few people about this, this will void your warranty and HooToo will have no responsibility to replace it.

**WARNING: (July 2020) The latest builds use a two step lzma loader process, to get around the 1.5 MB kernel limit on this device. However, one of the consequences of this is that modifying U-Boot variables will cause an error in the rootfs (as U-Boot will overwrite a portion of this partition). The device can be recovered, by re-flashing via the process below, using the factory.bin image.**


----

**Latest Firmware (21.02.0-22.03.0) (October 2022)**

Now that there is a release specific for the HT-TM05, this is the method for installing:
  - Download ''KERNEL-SQUASHFS'' and ''ROOTFS'' from here [[https://firmware-selector.openwrt.org/?version=22.03.0&target=ramips%2Fmt7620&id=hootoo_ht-tm05|Link]].
  - Rename the KERNEL-SQUASHFS file to ''kernel'' (no extension)
  - Rename the ROOTFS file to ''rootfs'' (no extension)
  - Place ''kernel'' and ''rootfs'' files in the root folder of the TFPT folder. For best results on Windows, use [[https://pjo2.github.io/tftpd64/|tftp64]].
  - Connect HT-TM05 to computer via ethernet cable.
  - Set computer IP to ''10.10.10.254'' subnet ''255.255.255.0''
  - Start the TFTP server. (If using Tftpd64, click ''Show Dir'' to make sure kernel/rootfs are shown. Then go to ''log viewer'' to see output from the process)
  - With your TM-HT05 turned off, hold the power button until you see lights then immediately release the power button and hold the reset button.
  - Continue holding reset until the files start transferring from the TFTP server.
  - The TM-HT05 will grab the kernel file and then the rootfs file and start flashing them. This takes some time 2-3 mins. 
  - After the TM-HT05 installs OpenWRT, it will reboot on it's own and when ready the indicator light will turn green.
  - Change your computer's IP back to Automatic IP Assignment/DHCP. (If that does not work, you can assign it 192.168.1.20, subnet 255.255.255.0)
  - OpenWRT defaults to ''192.168.1.1'', go to this address in your browser to access OpenWRT/Luci

Notes:

  * If using the serial connection from the PCB, the baudrate is now 57600
  * After this you will be able to update the firmware from the Luci/OpenWRT update page using the ''SYSUPGRADE'' file when the next OpenWRT firmware becomes available


----

**Chaos Calmer 15 Method**
Make sure you read through the entire list of steps before beginning. Video instructions are also available here: [[https://youtu.be/qPME1CLDT2o|External Link]]
  - Download this OpenWrt firmware: [[http://www.gl-inet.com/firmware/mt300n/clean/openwrt-gl-mt300n-clean-1.0.bin|Link]]
  - Place it in the root of a clean TFTP server running on your computer.
  - Rename the image to ''kernel'' — be sure there is no file extension, and that there are no other files on the TFTP server.
  - Plug the HT-TM05 into your computer via ethernet.
  - Set your computer to use ''10.10.10.254'' as its IP address.
  - With your HT-TM05 shut down, hold down the power button until the first white LED lights up.
  - Push and hold the reset button and release the power button. Continue holding the reset button for 30 seconds or until it begins searching for files on your TFTP server, whichever comes first.
  - The HT-TM05 will look for your computer at ''10.10.10.254'' and install the ''kernel'' file. Once it has finished installation of the ''kernel'' file, it will search for a (nonexistent) ''rootfs'' file — when it begins searching for this file, shut down the HT-TM05 by holding the power button normally.
  - Start up your HT-TM05 normally. When the Wi-Fi indicator turns green, OpenWrt has booted successfully.
OpenWrt uses ''192.168.1.1'' as the default router IP address, so you will need to switch your computer back to a dynamic IP address, or change your static IP address to ''192.168.1.X'' to configure OpenWrt.


===== Setting Up as a Wireless Repeater =====
**This can be done using [[https://github.com/openwrt/packages/blob/master/net/travelmate/files/README.md|travelmate]] addon or manually with the instructions below.
**

To setup the TM-HT05 to use as a wireless repeater (like the tripmate firmware worked) follow these instructions:
  - Go into ''Network->Wireless'' and press ''Scan'' on radio0
  - Select join on the Wifi you want to share
  - Check the box that says ''Replace Wireless Configuration''
  - Make sure ''Name of new network'' is ''wwan'' as well as the ''firewall-zone''
  - Save/Apply
  - Once connected to the wifi, select ''Add'' in the radio0 row
  - Towards the bottom on the ''General Setup'' tab make sure mode is ''Access Point''
  - Type the name you want for your wifi in ''ESSID''
  - Make sure the ''Network'' is ''lan''
  - You can add a passphrase in the ''Wireless Security'' tab
  - Save then Save/Apply

===== Unbricking (Advanced) =====
{{page>meta:infobox:dangerous&noheader&nofooter&noeditbtn}}

**WARNING!** This method involves opening the TM-HT05 and unsoldering the flash chip!
   - Disassembly instructions can be found on the original forum page [[https://forum.archive.openwrt.org/viewtopic.php?id=61445|here]] Post #18
   - The 8 pin flash chip below the index finger in [[https://dl2.pushbulletusercontent.com/iFJ0Ri9S96DQDr0rwkfEWYjbpalevBll/IMAG0117.jpg|this image]]
   - Unsolder chip from board. **Take care to remember the chip orientation!** Pin1 is marked with a faint circle on the top of the chip (This chip is a MX25L6406E which can be bought if a new one is needed)
   - Use a CH341A Programmer with a SOIC8 adapter socket ([[https://www.aliexpress.us/item/2255801161084769.html|Such as this kit]])
   - Place the chip in the socket adapter, making sure pin 1 of the chip is in the correct orientation
   - The socket adapter goes on the 25xx side of the latch on the CH341A Programmer (There are markings on the board showing the placement)
   - Install the CH341A drivers from [[https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1CHTNHqbuLMXEB1rS0m-mCeTF04uyT5m4?usp=sharing|here]]
   - Plug CH341A Programmer into computer
   - Open AsProgrammer, found [[https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1CHTNHqbuLMXEB1rS0m-mCeTF04uyT5m4?usp=sharing|here]]
   - In the ''IC'' menu choose ''SPI->MACRONIX->MX25L6406E''
   - Press the ''Read'' button to check if you are able to read from the chip
   - Open the original tripmate firmware file using the ''Open'' button, file found [[https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1CHTNHqbuLMXEB1rS0m-mCeTF04uyT5m4?usp=sharing|here]]
   - Press the arrow down next to the ''Program IC'' button and select the ''Unprotect->erase->program->verify'' button
   - The chip will start being flashed. This takes some time, around 10-15mins
   - If no errors, your chip is flashed
   - The chip can now be soldered back onto the device and will have the original tripmate firmware

